  <p>Strength coach Jordan Hoppel keeps nutrition simple and number-driven. Here is what he tells clients.</p>

  <h2>Protein: 1 gram per pound</h2>
  <p>A good proxy that's easy to follow: a gram of protein per pound of body weight. For fat loss, he also keeps protein high to hold onto muscle and to help stick to the diet, since protein keeps you full longer.</p>

  <h2>Calories for gaining muscle</h2>
  <p>Estimate maintenance calories, then increase by 300 to 500.</p>

  <h2>Protein timing</h2>
  <p>Jordan's answer on the post-workout window is blunt: it doesn't matter.</p>

  <h2>Get complete proteins</h2>
  <p>All animal-derived proteins are complete, with all essential and non-essential amino acids. The body can make some amino acids but not all, so you need complete sources (or the aminos on their own), but the body does better when they come together.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Plate of chicken, rice, and vegetables" caption="Protein plus rice or potatoes." brief="Photo: Jordan's typical meal, e.g. bison or beef with rice or potatoes." />

  <h2>Quick meals (10 minutes or less)</h2>
  <p>Beef, chicken, or bison with rice or potatoes.</p>

  <h2>Meal prep, the way Jordan does it</h2>
  <p>Jordan says he doesn't meal prep that often. When he does, he cooks and seasons proteins so they're quick to reheat, since protein takes time to cook, and uses rice that can be microwaved quickly.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Have food at home that you know you're eating that day.</JordanQuote>

  <p>His reasoning: if busy clients are unprepared and hungry at the end of the day, they grab the closest thing, often comfort food or fast food. Whey can help when protein is hard to hit, but you shouldn't be reliant on shakes; see <a href="/supplements-for-lifters/">supplements for lifters</a>.</p>
